%I A401676 #13 Nov 07 2026 09:47:43
%S A401676 1,1,4,9,16,25,81,81,256,256,1024,1024,4096,4096,16384,16384,65536,
%T A401676 65536,262144,262144,1048576,1048576,4194304,4194304,16777216,
%U A401676 16777216,67108864,67108864,268435456,268435456,1073741824,1073741824,4294967296,4294967296,17179869184
%N A401676 Maximum index of the center of a finite group with at most n pairwise noncommuting elements.
%C A401676 Let omega(G) denote the maximum cardinality of a set of pairwise noncommuting elements of a finite group G, and let Z(G) denote its center. Then a(n) = max{[G:Z(G)] : omega(G) <= n}.
%C A401676 The maximum is attained for every n. All groups attaining equality are classified up to isoclinism.
%C A401676 For n >= 9, the extremal groups are precisely those satisfying G' is isomorphic to C_2 and G/Z(G) is isomorphic to an elementary abelian 2-group of rank 2*floor((n-1)/2).
%C A401676 The same optimal bounds hold for arbitrary groups with finite omega(G).
%C A401676 The sequence gives the exact central-index profile associated with the bounded noncommutativity problem of B. H. Neumann.
%H A401676 Guillaume Lecomte, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.00943">The Exact Central-Index Profile of Groups with Bounded Noncommutativity</a>, arXiv:2611.00943 [math.GR], 2026.
%F A401676 a(n) = 1 for n = 1,2;
%F A401676 a(n) = (n-1)^2 for 3 <= n <= 6;
%F A401676 a(n) = 81 for n = 7,8;
%F A401676 a(n) = 4^floor((n-1)/2) for n >= 9.
%e A401676 For n = 7, an extraspecial group G of order 3^5 has [G:Z(G)] = 81 and omega(G) = 7. The central-index theorem shows that no finite group with omega(G) <= 7 has a larger central index. Hence a(7) = 81.
%o A401676 (Python)
%o A401676 def a(n):
%o A401676     if n <= 2:
%o A401676         return 1
%o A401676     if n <= 6:
%o A401676         return (n - 1)**2
%o A401676     if n <= 8:
%o A401676         return 81
%o A401676     return 4**((n - 1)//2)
%o A401676 print([a(n) for n in range(1, 36)])
%K A401676 nonn,easy,new
%O A401676 1,3
%A A401676 _Guillaume Lecomte_, Nov 01 2026
